.TH OMN 1 "2026-09" "Omniscient" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
omn \- one vocabulary for system observability
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B omn
.I source
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ target ]
.RI [ "\-\- program [args]" ]
.br
.B omn
.B \-f
.I session
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B omn list
.RI [ family | source ]
.br
.B omn validate
.B \-f
.I session
.br
.B omn formats
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B omn
describes a measurement along five axes and refuses the combinations that cannot
mean anything, rather than answering them with a number that means something
else.
.PP
It speaks to
.BR perf_event_open (2),
.BR bpf (2),
procfs, sysfs and netlink directly. It does not wrap
.BR perf (1),
.B bpftrace
or
.BR bcc ,
so there is no second output format to parse and no second clock in the middle
of the pipeline.
.SS The five axes
.TP
.B source
What to measure, as
.IR family . metric :
.BR io.latency ", " cpu.instructions ", " net.tcp.rtt .
.BR "omn list" " enumerates them."
.TP
.B target
What to observe:
.BR \-\-pid ", " \-\-tid ", " \-\-cgroup ", " \-\-cpu ", " \-\-iface ", "
.BR \-\-container ", " \-\-system ", or a program to launch after " \-\- .
.TP
.B mode
How to observe:
.BR count ", " sample@997hz ", " trace ", " poll@100ms ", " snapshot .
.TP
.B emit
What comes out:
.BR raw ", " hist ", " rate ", " topk ", " stack ", " timeline ", " summary .
.TP
.B keys
Correlation keys to attach:
.BR req ", " task ", " proc ", " file ", " sock ", " blk ", " mem ", " gpu ", "
.BR cgroup ", " iface .
.PP
A bare
.BI "omn " source
is short for
.BI "omn trace " source .
.SS What it refuses
The validator runs before anything is attached. It rejects a target a source
cannot carry, a mode it does not support, an emit that mode cannot produce, a
correlation key it does not carry, a filter field it does not expose, and an
output format that cannot express the requested emit. Each refusal says why and
what the source does accept.
.PP
Returning a machine-wide value while presenting it as per-process attribution is
the worst bug this tool could have, so the combination fails rather than
degrading.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "trace " source
Build a request, resolve it against this machine, and run it. The default
command, which may be omitted.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fIfamily\fR|\fIsource\fR]
Project the catalog. With no argument, every source; with a family, that family;
with a source, that entry in full, including every backend, what each one
requires and how each requirement was probed.
.IP
The listing answers three different questions in three columns.
.B HERE
is what this machine offers, probed now.
.B COLLECTOR
is what this build can measure, which is not the same question.
.B COST
is the cheapest backend in a chain, and
.B DET
is reproducibility between runs \(em never bit-exactness.
.TP
.B validate \fB\-f\fI session
Check a session against the catalog without touching the machine. Machine
independent, so it answers for a host other than this one.
.TP
.B formats
The input and output formats, which emits each output format can carry, and
which encoders exist yet.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Target
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " PID" "\fR, " \-\-tid " TID"
An existing process or thread. Repeatable as a comma-separated list.
.TP
.BI \-\-cgroup " PATH"
A cgroup v2 path.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu " SET"
Cores, written the way the kernel writes them:
.BR 0\-3,8 .
.TP
.BI \-\-iface " NAME"
A network interface.
.TP
.BI \-\-container " NAME"
A container, resolved to a cgroup plus a pid namespace.
.TP
.B \-\-system
The whole machine.
.TP
.BI \-\- " program " [ args ]
Launch the program and measure it. The program is held at a gate until every
collector is attached, so the measurement starts before its first instruction,
and the run ends when it does.
.SS Measurement
.TP
.BI \-m ", " \-\-mode " MODE"
.BR count ", " trace ", " snapshot ", " sample@\fIFREQ\fR ", " poll@\fIINTERVAL\fR.
A frequency is
.BR 997hz " or " 1khz ;
an interval is
.BR 100ms ", " 1s ", " 2m .
.TP
.BI \-e ", " \-\-emit " EMIT"
Comma-separated.
.BR hist " takes " :log2 " or " :linear: \fIWIDTH\fR,
and
.BR topk " takes " : \fIN\fR.
Asking for two emits costs one collection.
.TP
.BI \-k ", " \-\-keys " KEY"
Correlation keys to attach, comma-separated.
.TP
.BI \-\-filter " EXPR"
A predicate:
.RB ` "bytes > 4KiB && rwbs == 'R'" '.
Supports
.BR == " " != " " < " " <= " " > " " >= " " =~ " and " in ,
grouped with
.BR && ", " || ", " ! " and parentheses."
Integers take size suffixes
.RB ( 4KiB ", " 4KB )
and duration suffixes
.RB ( 5ms ).
.IP
The resolver pushes a predicate into the kernel where the backend allows it and
evaluates it here otherwise, and says which it did. A userspace filter is paid
for in full before it removes anything, and what it removed is reported beside
the losses.
.TP
.BI \-d ", " \-\-duration " DURATION"
How long to observe. Required unless a program is launched or the mode is
.BR snapshot .
.TP
.BI \-b ", " \-\-budget " PERCENT"
An overhead ceiling, as
.BR 2% .
Parsed and recorded; not yet enforced.
.TP
.BI \-\-stack " MODE"
.BR fp ", " dwarf ", " lbr " or " none .
.B dwarf
copies the stack on every sample: far more volume and cost than frame pointers.
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FORMAT"
.BR text " (default), " json ", " yaml ", " toml ", " ndjson ", " folded ", "
.BR speedscope ", " pprof ", " chrome .
A result format only accepts the emits it can express;
.B omn formats
says which, and which encoders exist.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-out " DIR"
Write the results and the resolved request into a directory. The resolved
request is always written as JSON whatever the results format is: results
without it are numbers whose provenance is gone.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-request
Print the request and how it resolves on this machine, then exit without
measuring.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-resolved
Print the resolved request alone \(em the record to keep beside the numbers.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-resolve
Do not consult this machine. For writing down a session meant to run somewhere
else, where what this machine can do says nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " WHEN"
.BR auto " (default), " always " or " never .
Colour is an accelerator and never the carrier of a fact: everything it
highlights is also written in words.
.SS Session files
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-file " PATH"
Read the session from a file, or from standard input with
.BR \- .
YAML, JSON and TOML are accepted, inferred from the extension.
.TP
.BI \-\-input\-format " FORMAT"
.BR yaml ", " json " or " toml ,
when the extension does not say or the input is standard input.
.SS Other
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " force:ID"
Pin a backend instead of resolving one. For the comparison bench: a result
obtained this way is not the result the tool would have produced, and the record
says so.
.TP
.BI \-\-catalog " DIR"
Load the catalog from a directory instead of the one compiled in. For iterating
on entries without rebuilding.
.SH SESSION FILES
A session file describes exactly what a command line does, and
.B \-\-dump\-request
prints one. The two surfaces cannot express different things, which is what
keeps them from drifting apart.
.PP
.RS
.EX
name: which allocation actually cost memory
duration: 30s
format: text

requests:
  \- label: ranges
    source: mem.heap.alloc
    target: {launch: [./server]}
    mode: trace
    emit: [summary]

  \- label: pages
    source: mem.faults
    target: {launch: [./server]}
    mode: trace
    emit: [rate]

joins:
  \- id: pages\-of\-allocation
    from: mem.heap.alloc
    to: mem.faults
    via: mem
    match: interval
    opens: "op == 'alloc'"
    closes: "op == 'free'"
    window: 60s
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every request in one session observes one target over one window. Running them
one after another would measure several different moments and present them as
one, and a join across that would correlate a program with its own past.
.SH EVIDENCE
Every correlated result carries the grounds it was obtained on, because a
correlation backed by a propagated identity and one backed by two things
happening at the same time are both "correlations" and only one is evidence.
.TP
.B IDENTITY
The downstream event carries an identity the upstream one propagated.
.TP
.B KEY
Both sides carry the same typed key.
.TP
.B WAKEUP
A wakeup chain links them: the only join that crosses a block.
.TP
.B TEMPORAL
They overlapped in time. A hypothesis, nothing more.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The measurement ran.
.TP
.B 1
Usage, parsing, or a failure while running.
.TP
.B 2
The request was refused: it cannot mean what it asks for, or this machine cannot
serve it.
.TP
.B 3
The request is valid and nothing collects it yet.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OMN_CATALOG_DIR
Load the catalog from this directory instead of the one compiled in.
.TP
.B OMN_PRELOAD_LIB
Path to the heap shim, when it is not beside the binary.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set and non-empty, or
.B TERM=dumb
, disables colour.
.SH PRIVILEGES
The eBPF backends need
.B CAP_BPF
and
.BR CAP_PERFMON ,
and several sysfs counters need root. An unprivileged run is refused with the
capability that is missing, never resolved onto a lesser backend that answers a
different question.
.PP
File capabilities do not survive a relink, so every rebuild drops them and
nothing announces it: the tool reports fewer sources as available, which reads
like the machine changed rather than the binary.
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo setcap cap_bpf,cap_perfmon+ep $(which omn)
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I catalog/*.yaml
The vocabulary, as data. Adding a source is an entry here, not code.
.TP
.I <out>/resolved.json
The backend selected, every requirement and how it was probed, what was
degraded, and the machine's kernel, governor, THP mode, SMT state and
clocksource. Two measurements taken through different backends are not
comparable, and this is what says which was used.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Resident memory of a program, as it runs:
.EX
omn mem.rss \-\-emit timeline \-\-mode poll@100ms \-\- ./build.sh
.EE
.TP
Which allocation actually cost memory:
.EX
omn \-f examples/allocation\-cost.yml
.EE
.TP
Round\-trip time of every TCP connection this machine holds:
.EX
omn net.tcp.rtt \-\-system \-m poll@1s \-d 30s \-e timeline
.EE
.TP
Instructions retired by a program, the most reproducible number in the catalog:
.EX
omn cpu.instructions \-m count \-e summary \-\- ./bench
.EE
.TP
Block I/O over 4 KiB, as a histogram:
.EX
omn io.latency \-\-system \-m trace \-e hist \-\-filter "bytes > 4KiB"
.EE
.TP
What this machine can serve, and what this build can measure:
.EX
omn list
.EE
.TP
Write a session down for a machine other than this one:
.EX
omn io.latency \-\-pid 1 \-m trace \-e hist \-\-no\-resolve \-\-dump\-request \-\-format yaml
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR perf_event_open (2),
.BR bpf (2),
.BR proc (5),
.BR sysfs (5),
.BR capabilities (7)
.PP
.IR CATALOG.md " for the vocabulary, " STATE.md " for what works today,"
.IR DECISIONS.md " for what is settled and why."
